<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"><channel><title>Open-Source on metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/tags/open-source/</link><description>Research Engineer &amp;amp; Computer Scientist - Machine Learning, Statistical Computing, Open Source Development</description><language>en-us</language><managingEditor>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</managingEditor><webMaster>lex@metafunctor.com (Alex Towell)</webMaster><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Jul 2026 08:44:23 -0500</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://queelius.github.io/tags/open-source/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><image><url>https://queelius.github.io/images/og-image.jpg</url><title>metafunctor</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/</link></image><item><title>The MCP Pattern: SQLite as the AI-Queryable Cache</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-20-the-mcp-pattern/</link><pubDate>Fri, 20 Mar 2026 20:39:07 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-20-the-mcp-pattern/</guid><description>Every data silo follows the same architecture: domain files as ground truth, SQLite + FTS5 as a read-only cache, MCP as the query interface. After building five of these, the pattern is clear.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-03-20-the-mcp-pattern/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>MCP</category><category>sqlite</category><category>architecture</category><category>cli</category><category>python</category><category>software-design</category><category>open-source</category><category>unix-philosophy</category></item><item><title>Narrating a Hugo Blog with Sentence Highlighting</title><link>https://queel