 medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>posthumous</category><category>dead-mans-switch</category><category>python</category><category>asyncio</category><category>totp</category><category>federation</category><category>open-source</category></item><item><title>A Map of My Open Source Ecosystem</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-07-ecosystem-overview/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-07-ecosystem-overview/</guid><description>A guided tour through my open-source ecosystem: encrypted search theory, statistical reliability, Unix-philosophy CLI tools, AI research, and speculative fiction. How the projects connect and where to start.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-07-ecosystem-overview/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>ecosystem</category><category>open-source</category><category>overview</category><category>oblivious-computing</category><category>statistics</category><category>reliability</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>unix-philosophy</category><category>digital-legacy</category><category>AI</category><category>LLM</category><category>R</category><category>C++</category><category>Python</category></item><item><title>pagevault: Hiding an Encryption Platform Inside HTML</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-13-pagevault/</link><pubDate>Fri, 13 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-13-pagevault/</guid><description>pagevault turns any file into a self-contained encrypted HTML page. No backend, no JavaScript libraries, no external dependencies. Just AES-256-GCM and the browser&amp;#39;s built-in Web Crypto API. The interesting part is making it work at scale.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-02-13-pagevault/dashboard-locked.png" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>pagevault</category><category>cryptoid</category><category>encryption</category><category>static-sites</category><category>python</category><category>web-crypto</category><category>open-source</category></item><item><title>hypothesize: Now on CRAN</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-12-hypothesize-cran/</link><pubDate>Fri, 12 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-12-hypothesize-cran/</guid><description>My R package for hypothesis testing, hypothesize, is now available on CRAN.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-12-hypothesize-cran/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>R</category><category>statistics</category><category>hypothesis testing</category><category>CRAN</category><category>open source</category></item><item><title>Everything is a File: Virtual Filesystems for CLI Data Tools</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-20-vfs-cli-tools/</link><pubDate>Mon, 20 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-20-vfs-cli-tools/</guid><description>How I turned scattered data managers into navigable systems using virtual filesystems and POSIX commands.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-20-vfs-cli-tools/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>unix-philosophy</category><category>cli-tools</category><category>python</category><category>data-management</category><category>virtual-filesystem</category><category>software-design</category><category>open-source</category></item><item><title>Why I Build Comprehensively in Open Source</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-open-source-legacy/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 Nov 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-open-source-legacy/</guid><description>On building comprehensive open source software as value imprinting at scale, reproducible science, and leaving intellectual legacy under terminal constraints.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-open-source-legacy/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>open-source</category><category>legacy</category><category>reproducible-science</category><category>philosophy</category><category>software-design</category></item><item><title>Building R Packages for Statistical Inference</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-05-statistical-computing-r/</link><pubDate>Fri, 08 May 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-05-statistical-computing-r/</guid><description>I&amp;#39;m building R packages for reliability analysis, not just using other people&amp;#39;s. R&amp;#39;s strengths for statistical computing are real, and building packages forces you to understand the theory.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2020-05-statistical-computing-r/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>R</category><category>statistics</category><category>statistical-computing</category><category>open-source</category></item><item><title>Why Open Source Matters for Reproducible Science</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2018-09-open-source-reproducible-science/</link><pubDate>Tue, 18 Sep 2018 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2018-09-open-source-reproducible-science/</guid><description>Code is a scientific artifact. If you don&amp;#39;t publish it, you&amp;#39;re hiding your methodology.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2018-09-open-source-reproducible-science/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Open-Source</media:title></media:content><category>open-source</category><category>reproducible-science</category><category>research</category><category>philosophy</category></item></channel></rss>